---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **「<작품> hit or flop」에 답하는 자리.** (`/hit-or-flop`)
 *
 * ── 왜 (2026-08-29) ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * Search Console 실측에서 `hit or flop` 을 붙여 물어본 질의가 **아홉 개**였다(노출 15회, 4~10위).
 * 우리 2,700여 장 가운데 **그 물음 «모양»에 답하는 지면이 없었다.** 작품별 지면은 수를 보여
 * 주지만 견줄 «분포»가 없어서 「그래서 이게 큰 건가」에는 답하지 못한다.
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 «하지 않는» 것 ───────────────────────────────
 * 「hit」·「flop」은 판정어다. 우리는 판정하지 않는다.
 *   ⛔ 「이건 flop 이다」            우리가 값어치를 매기는 것이다
 *   ✅ 「974편 중 447편이 한 나라뿐이었다」  사실이다. 판단은 읽는 사람이 한다
 * ⭐ 이 지면이 주는 것은 **자리**다 — 한 작품이 974편의 분포 «어디»에 있는지.
 *
 * ── 🔴 이 지면이 말하는 하나 ──────────────────────────────────
 * **「넷플릭스 1위」는 드물지 않다 — 974편 중 304편(31%)이 어딘가에서 1위를 했다.**
 * 드문 것은 «넓이»다: 다섯 나라를 넘긴 것이 22%뿐이다.
 * ⚠ 그러니 「1위 했다」로 크기를 재면 거의 아무것도 안 갈린다. 이것이 리스크관리의 자리다.
 *
 * ⛔ 화면에 한국어를 내지 않는다 — 손님은 영어권이다. 이 주석은 frontmatter 라 나가지 않는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwMethodLimits from '../../components/KcwMethodLimits.astro';
import data from '../../data/kcw-hit-or-flop.json';

const TITLE = 'Hit or flop? What Netflix’s charts actually recorded for 974 Korean titles';
const DESC = 'People ask whether a Korean film or series was a hit or a flop. We will not rule on '
  + 'that, and Netflix publishes nothing that would let anyone rule on it. What we can do is put a '
  + 'title next to the other 973: 46% reached exactly one country, and 31% were number one somewhere.';

const 나라 = data.distribution.countries;
const 주 = data.distribution.weeks;
const 몫 = (a: number, b: number) => Math.round((a / b) * 100);
const 널 = (v: number | null | undefined) => (v === null || v === undefined ? '—' : v.toLocaleString('en-US'));
const 큰띠 = Math.max(...나라.bands.map((b: { titles: number }) => b.titles),
  ...주.bands.map((b: { titles: number }) => b.titles));

const 찾은것 = data.asked.filter((a: any) => a.found);

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  spatialCoverage: `${data.marketCount} countries`,
  variableMeasured: ['Countries reached', 'Weeks on chart', 'Peak rank'],
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/hit-or-flop">
  <h1>Hit or flop?</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    We are not going to tell you. Netflix publishes no budget, no revenue and no viewing hours by
    country, so nobody working from this data can tell you either &mdash; and we would rather say
    that plainly than dress a rank up as a verdict.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    What we can do is give you the thing the word &ldquo;flop&rdquo; is really reaching for:
    {' '}<b>somewhere to stand.</b> Between {data.weekFrom} and {data.weekTo},
    {' '}<b>{data.titleCount.toLocaleString('en-US')} Korean titles</b> entered a country&rsquo;s
    Netflix weekly top 10 at least once. Here is how far each of them got, and where the titles
    people ask about sit inside that.
  </p>

  <p class="warn">
    <b>&ldquo;Number one on Netflix&rdquo; is not the rare part.</b>
    {' '}<b>{data.everNumberOne} of the {data.titleCount.toLocaleString('en-US')} titles
    &mdash; {몫(data.everNumberOne, data.titleCount)}% &mdash; have been number one in some country
    in some week.</b> Number one in one small market for one week is common. What separates titles
    is how many countries they reached and how long they stayed, and on both of those the spread is
    enormous: from one country for one week to {나라.max} countries for {주.max} weeks.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>How many countries a Korean title reaches</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Countries that ever charted it</th><th class="num">Titles</th><th class="num">Share</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {나라.bands.map((b: { label: string; titles: number; pct: number }) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{b.label}</td>
            <td class="num">{b.titles.toLocaleString('en-US')}
              <span class="bar" style={`--w:${Math.round((b.titles / 큰띠) * 100)}%`}></span>
            </td>
            <td class="num">{b.pct}%</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fine">
      <b>{나라.bands[0].titles} titles &mdash; {나라.bands[0].pct}% of everything that ever charted
      &mdash; reached exactly one country and nowhere else.</b> The middle title reached
      {' '}{널(나라.p50)} countries. You need {널(나라.p90)} countries to be in the top tenth, and
      {' '}{널(나라.p99)} to be in the top hundredth.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      This is a distribution, not a standard. There is no number on this table that a title is
      supposed to hit. Most of what Netflix carries charts narrowly, and that is what narrow
      looks like &mdash; not a failure of anything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How long a Korean title stays on a chart</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Distinct weeks with a place somewhere</th><th class="num">Titles</th><th class="num">Share</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {주.bands.map((b: { label: string; titles: number; pct: number }) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{b.label}</td>
            <td class="num">{b.titles.toLocaleString('en-US')}
              <span class="bar" style={`--w:${Math.round((b.titles / 큰띠) * 100)}%`}></span>
            </td>
            <td class="num">{b.pct}%</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fine">
      The middle title held a place for {널(주.p50)} weeks. {data.oneWeekOnly} titles
      ({몫(data.oneWeekOnly, data.titleCount)}%) appeared for a single week and never again, and
      {' '}<b>{data.oneCountryOneWeek} titles were on one chart, in one country, for one week</b>
      {' '}&mdash; that is the whole record we hold for them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The titles people ask this about</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      These {data.asked.length} are not our pick. They are the titles that people actually typed
      &ldquo;hit or flop&rdquo; after and then landed on this site, taken from our own Search
      Console figures for the four weeks to 2026-08-26. We found chart rows for
      {' '}{data.askedFound} of them.
    </p>
    <table class="asked">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Title</th><th class="num">Countries</th><th class="num">Weeks</th>
          <th class="num">Best rank</th><th class="num">Wider than</th><th>Charted</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {찾은것.map((a: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">
              {a.hasPage ? <a href={`/title/${a.slug}`}>{a.title}</a> : <span>{a.title}</span>}
              <span class="fine"> {a.type === 'Films' ? 'film' : 'series'}</span>
            </td>
            <td class="num"><b>{a.markets}</b></td>
            <td class="num">{a.weeks}</td>
            <td class="num">{a.peak ?? '—'}</td>
            <td class="num">{a.pctCountries === null ? '—' : `${a.pctCountries}%`}</td>
            <td class="fine">{a.firstWeek} to {a.lastWeek}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="fine">
      <b>&ldquo;Wider than&rdquo;</b> is the share of all {data.titleCount.toLocaleString('en-US')}
      {' '}charting Korean titles that reached fewer countries than this one. It is a position in
      the pack, and nothing more. It is not a score, it does not go up when a title is good, and
      two titles a percentage point apart are not meaningfully different.
    </p>
    {data.askedMissing > 0 && (
      <p class="fine">
        <b>{data.askedMissing} of the titles asked about are not in our table at all.</b> Either
        they never entered a country weekly top 10 in the weeks we hold, or Netflix listed them
        under a name we did not match. We would rather leave the row out than guess at it.
      </p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>For scale: the widest {data.widest.length}</h2>
    <p class="sub">The far end of the same distribution, so the numbers above have a ceiling to sit under.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Title</th><th class="num">Countries</th><th class="num">Weeks</th><th class="num">Best rank</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.widest.map((r: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">{r.hasPage ? <a href={`/title/${r.slug}`}>{r.title}</a> : <span>{r.title}</span>}</td>
            <td class="num"><b>{r.markets}</b></td>
            <td class="num">{r.weeks}</td>
            <td class="num">{r.peak ?? '—'}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What this page cannot answer</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The word &ldquo;flop&rdquo; usually means money. None of the four things below are in this
      data, and no arrangement of chart ranks will produce them.
    </p>
    <ul class="cant">
      {data.cannotAnswer.map((c: string) => <li>{c}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      This is a statistic, not a judgement of a film, a cast, or anyone&rsquo;s work. If a title you
      care about sits at the narrow end, that is where its chart rows sit &mdash; it is not a
      statement about the title, and we are not making one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to walk from here</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/netflix-korea-this-week">The Korean titles on this week&rsquo;s charts</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/underrated">The ones that ran and ran, almost nowhere</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/most-popular">Three measures of &ldquo;most popular&rdquo; that disagree</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/korean-movies-on-netflix">Korean films, counted separately</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/by-country">Every country, and how Korean its chart is</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/staying-power">How long titles hold a place</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/titles">Every Korean title we hold</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwMethodLimits
    method={`Netflix's own weekly top 10 files by country, ${data.weekFrom} to ${data.weekTo}, across ${data.marketCount} countries and ${data.weekCount} weeks. Countries counts every market that ever listed the title. Weeks counts distinct weeks in which it held a place anywhere, so a title in three countries in the same week counts that week once. The nine titles in the middle table come from this site's own Google Search Console query export for the four weeks to 2026-08-26, not from an editorial choice. Netflix publishes films and series as two separate weekly lists; a name that appears on both is two different works and we do not add them together.`}
    limitation={`A chart place is a rank against other titles in one country in one week. It is not a viewing figure, an audience count or a revenue figure, and a small market counts exactly the same as a large one here. Netflix publishes no budget and no revenue, so this page cannot say whether anything made money, which is what "hit or flop" usually means. Titles that never entered any country's weekly top 10 leave no row at all, so this data cannot tell "reached few people" apart from "not in our files". Netflix states no country of production; which titles count as Korean is our inference from the title text and from Wikidata, and we correct it when we find it wrong.`}
  />

  <style>
    .lead { font-size: 1.08rem; }
    .warn { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: .8rem 1rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 2rem;
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 6%, transparent); }
    .sub { color: var(--muted); font-size: .95rem; margin: .2rem 0 1rem; }
    .fine { color: var(--muted); font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.6; }
    section { margin: 2.4rem 0; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .95rem; margin-bottom: .8rem; }
    th, td { padding: .45rem .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); text-align: left;
      vertical-align: baseline; }
    th { font-weight: 600; font-size: .82rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em;
      color: var(--muted); }
    .num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap; }
    .nm { min-width: 12rem; }
    .bar { display: block; height: 3px; margin-top: .25rem; width: var(--w);
      background: var(--accent); opacity: .55; margin-left: auto; }
    .asked tbody tr:hover { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 5%, transparent); }
    .cant { color: var(--muted); font-size: .92rem; line-height: 1.65; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
    .cant li { margin-bottom: .5rem; }
    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      table { font-size: .88rem; }
      .nm { min-width: 8rem; }
    }
  </style>
</WikiTip>
